1. Overpass QL Query StructureAn Overpass API query is written in Overpass QL (Query Language) and consists of three main structural parts:Settings Block: Defines the output format (JSON, XML, CSV) and execution constraints like timeouts.   Query Block: Selects geographical elements using spatial filters (bounding boxes) and key-value tag filters. You can isolate results into named sets using ->.set_name;.   Output Block: Specifies how the data is returned (e.g., out body;, out center; to get the centroid coordinate of building polygons, or out geom; for full geometries).   2. Available "Tables" and Commercial TagsOpenStreetMap is a spatial graph database rather than a relational SQL database. Instead of traditional tables, data consists of three fundamental elements annotated with Key-Value tags:Nodes: Point features (lat/lon coordinates).Ways: Linear features or closed polygons (buildings, property lines, commercial complexes).Relations: Groupings of nodes and ways that define complex geometries or administrative boundaries.   nwr shortcut: A shorthand command that queries nodes, ways, and relations simultaneously.   To capture physical businesses, you target specific commercial key-value pairs:office=*: Professional services, software firms, corporate HQs (e.g., office=company, office=it, office=administrative).shop=*: Retail stores, storefronts, local commercial outlets (e.g., shop=supermarket, shop=hardware, shop=computers).industrial=*: Manufacturing plants, logistics centers, warehouses (e.g., industrial=factory, industrial=warehouse).craft=*: Trade businesses, small workshops, contractors (e.g., craft=electrician, craft=plumber).amenity=*: Service providers, medical offices, financial institutions (e.g., amenity=bank, amenity=doctors, amenity=dentist).Filter Best Practice: Always append [name] to your tag queries to instantly drop anonymous points of interest like unlabelled utility boxes or nameless benches.3. Targeting Buffalo, NY and Surrounding AreasFor a multi-municipality pipeline covering Buffalo and directly adjacent areas, Bounding Boxes (BBOX) provide the most deterministic control. A bounding box is defined by four coordinates: [south, west, north, east].   The core query structure for a specific sector looks like this:Plaintext[out:json][timeout:30];
[bbox:42.82,-78.93,43.00,-78.75]; // Example: Greater Buffalo Bounding Box

(
  nwr["office"][name];
  nwr["shop"][name];
  nwr["industrial"][name];
  nwr["craft"][name];
  nwr["amenity"~"bank|clinic|doctors|dentist"][name];
);

out center;
4. Organizing into Geolocational SetsTo segment the output so job seekers can filter by exact local areas (such as Downtown Buffalo, Amherst, Cheektowaga, or Tonawanda) rather than sorting through a massive unified file, handle the partitioning at the script level rather than the API level.Define a Regional Dictionary in Python: Map out distinct bounding boxes for each target municipality or zip-code cluster.Iterate and Tag: Write your ingestion script to loop through each regional key, query the Overpass API sequentially, and inject a metadata tag (region: "Amherst" or zip: "14304") into the resulting JSON objects.Compile Structured Markdown: Group the processed entries under distinct regional markdown headings or separate files so users can isolate their local search radius instantly.
